WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.967
The practice database for this module is a small synthetic healthcare database.

00:00:04.967 --> 00:00:08.477
The patients table contains one row per patient.

00:00:08.477 --> 00:00:14.502
Admissions, lab results, and prescriptions are child tables, which means a single patient can have several

00:00:14.502 --> 00:00:17.527
related rows in each of those tables.

00:00:17.527 --> 00:00:20.397
We also have medical staff and departments.

00:00:20.397 --> 00:00:26.257
Staff members belong to departments, and admissions can reference an attending staff member.

00:00:26.257 --> 00:00:32.332
These relationships let us practice both simple joins and multi-table join paths.

00:00:32.332 --> 00:00:36.087
The database intentionally contains useful edge cases.

00:00:36.087 --> 00:00:41.072
There is a patient with no admission activity, a department with no activity,

00:00:41.072 --> 00:00:46.167
and a staging record whose patient ID does not appear in the official patient master.

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:52.277
Those cases help us see the difference between INNER, LEFT, RIGHT, and FULL OUTER joins.

00:00:52.277 --> 00:00:58.117
As you work through the examples, remember that repeated values are not automatically errors.

00:00:58.117 --> 00:01:05.742
If one patient has two admissions, the patient information may legitimately appear twice in an admission-level

00:01:05.742 --> 00:01:09.612
result. The key question is always what one output row represents.
